{"_meta":{"anthropic/maxResultSizeChars":200000},"annotations":{"destructiveHint":true,"idempotentHint":false,"openWorldHint":true,"readOnlyHint":true},"description":"Smoke-play Eligian programs at runtime: compile each file and play every timeline on the real Eligius engine (headless jsdom), then replay an optional scenario (click, key, seek, navigate, wait, snapshot) and check its assertions. It runs in a child process (`eligian smoke --format ndjson`). A full presentation can take 40 s or more: pass `paths` for one file, or `timelines` for a subset. After `timeoutMs` (default 45000) the child is stopped and the result holds what was played so far, with `partial: true`. `ok: true` means no runtime error and not partial; with `strict` (the default) a selectElement that matches nothing is an error. `determinism: true` compares linear play, a straight seek and a seek back from the end at every boundary; about three times slower, so pass `timelines` for a subset. A scenario is an array of steps, `{ steps, assertions }`, or the path of a scenario JSON file, and applies to one program file. Steps: `{ \"seek\": <s> }`, `{ \"click\": \"<selector>\" }`, `{ \"key\": \"<key>\", \"in\"?: \"<selector>\" }`, `{ \"navigate\": \"<timeline>\" }`, `{ \"wait\": <ms> }`, `{ \"snapshot\": \"<label>\" }`. Each step returns the active timeline, the position and a snapshot of every layout id (exists, classes, text, style, visible, controllers) plus `activeTimelineActions`; concise steps carry only the entries that changed since the previous step, `response_format: \"detailed\"` the full snapshots. Assertions: `{ after: <snapshot label | step index>, selector?: \"#<id>\", hasClass | notHasClass | text | visible | controllers | timeline | position }`, where `position` is seconds, `{ sameAs: <step> }` or `{ greaterThan: <step> }`; a failed one is a `scenario-assertion-failed` problem with `expected` and `actual`. Security: the program's extensions module (`host.ts`) is user code; it is exec